WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.010 --> 00:00:04.960
Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 2 is stacked
with easter eggs, new characters and heart-breaking

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:09.590
emotional moments, but numerous scenes and
characters still wound up on the cutting room

00:00:09.590 --> 00:00:10.590
floor.

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:13.400
Yippee-ki-yay movie lovers, it's Jan here
and in this video, I'm going to be breaking

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:17.480
down a ton of incredible concept art which
features alternate designs and characters

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:21.990
we never got to see in Guardians Vol 2, and
I'll also be analysing all the scenes and

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:24.460
characters that were deleted from the final
cut.

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:28.670
A quick reminder that my Guardians of the
Galaxy giveaway is still running, so be sure

00:00:28.670 --> 00:00:32.930
to leave a comment about the movie and subscribe
for a chance to win and I'll provide more

00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:34.719
details at the end of the video.

00:00:34.719 --> 00:00:37.920
Obviously, spoilers lie ahead for Guardians
of the Galaxy Vol. 2!

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:41.670
If you haven't seen the movie yet, you can
tap in the top-right for my spoiler-free review

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:44.590
and come back after you've seen the film.

00:00:44.590 --> 00:00:49.870
In Vol. 2, to differentiate Mantis from Gamora,
James Gunn decided not to give the new character

00:00:49.870 --> 00:00:52.420
green skin, which she often has in the comics.

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:56.709
However, various pieces of concept art for
Mantis show a green-skinned Mantis was definitely

00:00:56.709 --> 00:01:02.679
considered at one point, with reds, blues,
purples and many other shades also considered.

00:01:02.679 --> 00:01:06.120
The antennae for Mantis in the concept art
are also a bit more bug-like and her costumes

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:10.010
are more detailed than the final smooth costume
that Mantis ended up with.

00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:14.320
In fact, according to visual effects supervisor
Chris Townsend, they adopted a very subtle

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:18.080
approach when it came to using CGI to enhance
Mantis's look.

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:22.080
Townsend said that, "[Mantis] is supposed
to be bug-like, so we talked about larger

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:25.230
eyes, strange mandibles and various prosthetics.

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:28.970
We played around with lots of different ideas
[… but] we eventually ended up realizing

00:01:28.970 --> 00:01:33.140
that Pom's performance is so beautiful [and
subtle], that all we really needed to do was

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:38.830
put the antennae on her head and give her
black irises to give her more of a bug feeling."

00:01:38.830 --> 00:01:42.640
Marvel's Visual Development team for Vol.2
also considered many options for the look

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:45.210
of Ego before settling on his final form.

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:49.300
His uniqueness as a character meant that everything
from his human appearance, to his ship and

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:52.000
his planet needed to all work together.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:56.890
Visual Development Supervisor Andy Park says
that ideas for Ego's human form ranged from

00:01:56.890 --> 00:02:01.470
a "swashbuckling pirate" to a "gunslinging
cowboy" to more "organic earthy" looks.

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:05.520
In the end, they went for a "space frontiersmen
mixed with a little bit of western", which

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:08.289
seemed to fit well with his final role in
the movie.

00:02:08.289 --> 00:02:13.400
So for Ego's costume, designer Judianna Makovsky
used many layers of different leathers that

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:17.670
were laser-etched and dyed to give them what
she called a sort of medieval or renaissance

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:18.670
detail.

00:02:18.670 --> 00:02:23.170
As for Ego's planet, that's home to a couple
of deleted scenes, and in the first one, just

00:02:23.170 --> 00:02:27.730
after the Guardians arrive there, Ego shows
Peter a statue he made of Star-Lord and his

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:28.730
friends.

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:31.639
Director James Gunn said it was a really great
joke, but he ended up cutting it because it

00:02:31.639 --> 00:02:33.719
didn't move the plot along.

00:02:33.719 --> 00:02:37.999
And inside Ego's palace there was another
cut scene where Ego shifted the appearance

00:02:37.999 --> 00:02:39.069
of a whole room.

00:02:39.069 --> 00:02:43.980
In the scene, the wall panels reformed into
a type of sand mural that showed various touching

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:48.159
moments from Peter's life, such as time he
spent with his mother and scenes of him bonding

00:02:48.159 --> 00:02:49.810
with Yondu and Groot.

00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:54.090
Like the statue scene, this was another way
for Ego to show how he admired Peter and had

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:57.529
been taking an interest in his son throughout
his life.

00:02:57.529 --> 00:03:01.799
Something we didn't get to see in the final
movie was the Sovereign's Envoy Robots.

00:03:01.799 --> 00:03:06.019
These seem to be a kind of soldier robot which
is piloted remotely by the Sovereign people,

00:03:06.019 --> 00:03:09.980
in a similar way to how they operated their
Golden Drones remotely when they attacked

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:11.629
the Guardians in space.

00:03:11.629 --> 00:03:15.260
It's interesting to speculate on what scenes
these robots could have been involved in,

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:19.139
perhaps, for example, they were part of some
backstory involving the problems the Sovereign

00:03:19.139 --> 00:03:21.220
had with the Anulax Batteries.

00:03:21.220 --> 00:03:25.220
What we do know for sure about the Envoy Robots,
however, is that, according to visual development

00:03:25.220 --> 00:03:29.989
artist Rodney Fuentebella, their design mixes
the gold colouring of the Sovereign race with

00:03:29.989 --> 00:03:33.309
an homage to the look of B-movie alien robots.

00:03:33.309 --> 00:03:38.129
Nathan Fillion has been a regular in many
of James Gunn's previous films including Slither,

00:03:38.129 --> 00:03:42.450
Super, and the first Guardians of the Galaxy
movie where he voiced the Monstrous Inmate

00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:47.810
at the Kyln prison who threatened Peter Quill
until Groot took matters into his own hands.

00:03:47.810 --> 00:03:52.579
As for Guardians Vol. 2, there was an extended
scene set on Earth that had the Firefly star

00:03:52.579 --> 00:03:56.659
appear in what was likely going to be a funny
Easter Egg-style cameo.

00:03:56.659 --> 00:04:00.949
Set photos indicate there were scenes filmed
of a Simon Williams Film Festival where Nathan

00:04:00.949 --> 00:04:04.799
Fillion appeared as the actor Simon Williams
on posters at the festival.

00:04:04.799 --> 00:04:08.129
The scene would have been filled with easter
eggs because Simon Williams is actually a

00:04:08.129 --> 00:04:12.590
character from the comics who's been an actor,
a supervillain, and superhero, [and who's]

00:04:12.590 --> 00:04:14.709
also known as Wonder Man.

00:04:14.709 --> 00:04:18.360
On top of that, pictures of the posters from
the set suggest that the films showing at

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:20.730
the festival were going to be spoofs of some
well-known movies.

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:26.270
For example, there was "Dead Before Arrival",
a riff on the poster for Inception; "Oh, Rebecca!",

00:04:26.270 --> 00:04:31.180
which looks like a spoof of the rom-com "Definitely,
Maybe" which starred Ryan Reynolds; "Haxan

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:36.010
2" that looks like the poster for the Daniel
Radcliffe movie "Horns"; and Tony Stark, which

00:04:36.010 --> 00:04:40.870
clearly takes its inspiration from Danny Boyle's
biopic of Steve Jobs.

00:04:40.870 --> 00:04:44.320
And during those missing sequences on Earth,
there was also going to be a longer scene

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:48.100
with Grandpa Quill in which, according to
an interview with Collider, James Gunn said

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:51.570
there would be even more easter eggs than
the scenes in the Collector's museum in the

00:04:51.570 --> 00:04:53.190
first Guardians movie.

00:04:53.190 --> 00:04:58.500
Grandpa Quill did appear very briefly in a
car pushed along by the blob, but Gunn drastically

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:03.040
cut the Earth scenes losing most of Grandpa
Quill plus numerous other cameos by friends

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:04.840
of the director.

00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:08.320
Another planned cameo which, sadly, didn't
happen was by David Bowie.

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:12.470
During Vol. 2's development, both Kevin Feige
and James Gunn had reached out to see if they

00:05:12.470 --> 00:05:15.740
could get a cameo with the singer and at one
point it was looking hopeful.

00:05:15.740 --> 00:05:20.870
Gunn had also secured the rights to use another
of Bowie's songs in Vol. 2 after having already

00:05:20.870 --> 00:05:23.460
used "Moonage Daydream" in the first film.

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:27.620
However, the news suddenly broke that Bowie
had died which gutted Gunn as he was a huge

00:05:27.620 --> 00:05:29.240
fan of the musical legend.

00:05:29.240 --> 00:05:33.680
Unfortunately, the scene in which Gunn planned
to use the Bowie track was also cut so we

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:36.000
never got to hear it in the final film.

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:40.960
There was also a major character Gunn initially
planned to use in Vol 2, but who was removed

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:42.450
from the final script.

00:05:42.450 --> 00:05:46.870
That character was Adam Warlock, who Gunn
told Slashfilm is one of his favourite characters

00:05:46.870 --> 00:05:49.220
and was going to be a big part of Vol. 2.

00:05:49.220 --> 00:05:53.400
However, the director felt there were too
many characters in the screenplay, so he decided

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:57.860
to lose Warlock instead of deleting Mantis,
who he felt was "more organically part of

00:05:57.860 --> 00:05:58.860
the movie".

00:05:58.860 --> 00:06:02.610
At least, we still got that lovely tease for
Adam Warlock in the end-credits scene with

00:06:02.610 --> 00:06:06.280
Ayesha, and if you want to find out about
the importance of Adam Warlock's role in the

00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:10.520
Marvel Cinematic Universe going forward, tap
the top-right of the screen to watch a video

00:06:10.520 --> 00:06:12.630
I made all about that.

00:06:12.630 --> 00:06:16.650
There were also a few jokes teased in trailers
and promo material that didn't make it into

00:06:16.650 --> 00:06:17.650
the final movie.

00:06:17.650 --> 00:06:21.760
There was a great "I Am Groot" moment from
the trailer in the "Death Button" scene with

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:24.650
Rocket which I was sorry not to see in the
final cut.

00:06:24.650 --> 00:06:26.541
"You have an atomic bomb in your bag, if anybody's
gonna have tape it's you!"

00:06:26.541 --> 00:06:27.541
"I have to do everything!"

00:06:27.541 --> 00:06:28.541
"You are wasting a lot of time!"

00:06:28.541 --> 00:06:29.541
"I am Groooooooot!"

00:06:29.541 --> 00:06:37.430
"That's a really bad sign."

00:06:37.430 --> 00:06:41.810
And James Gunn also shared a funny deleted
scene of the team eating around a campfire

00:06:41.810 --> 00:06:47.870
with Drax slurping noisily to everyone else's
disgust.

00:06:47.870 --> 00:06:51.140
"Dude!"

00:06:51.140 --> 00:06:54.410
"What?"

00:06:54.410 --> 00:06:57.680
"Dude!"

00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:00.950
"What?"

00:07:00.950 --> 00:07:05.340
Many consider Baby Groot to be a real scene-stealer
and there's some concept art of the little

00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:08.960
tree that's very likely to make you love him
even further.

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:13.530
Concept artist Josh Herman has released some
very cute artwork including a Baby Groot crying

00:07:13.530 --> 00:07:17.960
with a milk bottle, Groot with a toy Rocket
Racoon and another with the toddler unravelling

00:07:17.960 --> 00:07:20.840
Peter Quill's Awesome Mix Tape Vol. 1.

00:07:20.840 --> 00:07:26.090
There's some great concept art of Contraxia,
the icy planet where the Ravagers go to party,

00:07:26.090 --> 00:07:29.300
which shows us parts of the planet we didn't
get to see in the film.

00:07:29.300 --> 00:07:34.170
In the artwork by Craig Sellars, you can see
a kind of giant love bot submerged in a frozen

00:07:34.170 --> 00:07:38.930
lake with a railway running through, and there's
also what look like various industrial installations

00:07:38.930 --> 00:07:39.930
in the background.

00:07:39.930 --> 00:07:43.860
And there were also some interesting alternative
designs considered for the look of the love

00:07:43.860 --> 00:07:48.280
bots on Contraxia, until the final design
was settled on, which was inspired by Fritz

00:07:48.280 --> 00:07:50.340
Lang's film Metropolis.

00:07:50.340 --> 00:07:55.700
The many-tentacled Abilisk was a new monster
introduced to the MCU in Vol. 2, however some

00:07:55.700 --> 00:07:59.830
early designs for the creature show the beast
with feet and multiple tails rather than tentacles.

00:07:59.830 --> 00:08:05.020
By the way, inspiration for the Abilisk seems
to come from the comic book Cancerverse, and

00:08:05.020 --> 00:08:08.720
if you're interested in finding out more about
that, you can tap in the top right where I

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:11.810
talk about it in my Vol. 2 easter egg video.

00:08:11.810 --> 00:08:16.210
The Ravager clans were united by the end of
the movie under Stakar in a nice end-credits

00:08:16.210 --> 00:08:17.960
teaser for Vol. 3.

00:08:17.960 --> 00:08:21.620
Initially, Stakar's design was going to be
more alien-like, as you can see from this

00:08:21.620 --> 00:08:26.270
concept art by Rodney Fuentebella; however,
once Sylvester Stallone was cast in the role,

00:08:26.270 --> 00:08:30.370
the filmmakers didn't want to hide his presence
under a lot of prosthetics or CGI.

00:08:30.370 --> 00:08:35.170
So, focus turned, instead, to modifying the
character's costume rather than his face.

00:08:35.170 --> 00:08:39.229
For example, Stakar's energy wings that come
from his comic book character were also initially

00:08:39.229 --> 00:08:43.580
placed on his head until it was thought this
looked too strange, so they were re-located

00:08:43.580 --> 00:08:44.960
on his shoulders.

00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:49.150
Other members of Stakar's team include Mainframe,
who, in the movie, is basically a metal head

00:08:49.150 --> 00:08:52.710
that talks and is voiced by none other than
Miley Cyrus.

00:08:52.710 --> 00:08:56.510
The challenge for Mainframe's design was to
come up with a head that could then plug into

00:08:56.510 --> 00:08:59.800
other robots or objects for her character
to control.

00:08:59.800 --> 00:09:03.370
Various alternative designs were considered
for Mainframe's head before settling on the

00:09:03.370 --> 00:09:06.640
final one we see in the Vol. 2 end-credits
scene.

00:09:06.640 --> 00:09:11.440
The design of Martinex, the crystal-faced
alien played by Michael Rosenbaum, was particularly

00:09:11.440 --> 00:09:15.510
difficult as the reflections and refractions
of the light from the crystals caused various

00:09:15.510 --> 00:09:16.510
issues.

00:09:16.510 --> 00:09:21.750
So, some smoother and differently shaped heads
were tried before settling on his final look.

00:09:21.750 --> 00:09:26.060
For the appearance of the wormlike Krugarr,
Gunn wanted to ensure a comic book-style look

00:09:26.060 --> 00:09:30.070
and the designers kept his connection to Doctor
Strange with his hand gestures and use of

00:09:30.070 --> 00:09:31.070
glyphs.

00:09:31.070 --> 00:09:35.270
An alternate design of the character has him
looking more blinged-up and kinda creepier,

00:09:35.270 --> 00:09:39.020
although clearly this design didn't make it
to the final version.

00:09:39.020 --> 00:09:43.670
And not content with just five post-credit
scenes, Gunn nearly included a sixth scene

00:09:43.670 --> 00:09:48.000
which would have featured the Ravager Gef,
one of Taserface's mutineers.

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:51.950
In the final movie, Gef died when Yondu's
arrow killed him while he was drinking a glass

00:09:51.950 --> 00:09:52.950
of beer.

00:09:52.950 --> 00:09:57.060
However, in a deleted end-credits scene, Gef
was going to pop up again when Gamora and

00:09:57.060 --> 00:09:59.590
Mantis suddenly hear screaming on the ship.

00:09:59.590 --> 00:10:04.570
They discover that Gef has been on the ship
all along and is mortally wounded.

00:10:04.570 --> 00:10:07.940
Gunn said the scene felt confusing in the
end and didn't make a lot of sense, and that's

00:10:07.940 --> 00:10:09.730
why it was cut.

00:10:09.730 --> 00:10:13.050
During one of the jump scenes, there was a
nice tease for the Kronan character Korg who

00:10:13.050 --> 00:10:17.070
will be appearing in Thor Ragnarok, but concept
art for the scene shows that originally the

00:10:17.070 --> 00:10:21.390
design for the planet they were fighting on
was somewhat more detailed and included huge

00:10:21.390 --> 00:10:23.730
stone heads, hands and feet in the background.

00:10:23.730 --> 00:10:27.580
I guess the planet's look was simplified in
the end so we could focus on the two fighting

00:10:27.580 --> 00:10:29.800
Kronans in the super-brief scene.

00:10:29.800 --> 00:10:35.240
Now, we've got to talk about that ending in
Vol. 2 and how sad Yondu's heroic death was.

00:10:35.240 --> 00:10:38.990
In fact, that wasn't how the movie was going
to end orignally and in an interview with

00:10:38.990 --> 00:10:43.040
Uproxx, James Gunn said that "he didn't want
that to be the ending, and […] refused to

00:10:43.040 --> 00:10:44.810
put [it] in […] for a long time."

00:10:44.810 --> 00:10:48.770
And actually if you think back to the first
Guardians movie, Peter was rescued after he

00:10:48.770 --> 00:10:51.380
went into space to save Gamora from a similar
fate.

00:10:51.380 --> 00:10:56.150
So could there have been a way for Rocket
to rescue both Peter and Yondu in Vol. 2?

00:10:56.150 --> 00:11:00.330
Although I was very sad to lose Yondu, it
felt like it was a strong move for the film

00:11:00.330 --> 00:11:01.440
and the MCU in general.

00:11:01.440 --> 00:11:06.380
Indeed, Gunn has defended his decision saying
that "we need to have real stakes in these

00:11:06.380 --> 00:11:07.380
movies.

00:11:07.380 --> 00:11:08.380
We need to lose characters.

00:11:08.380 --> 00:11:10.810
And not everyone who sees Marvel movies loves
that."

00:11:10.810 --> 00:11:14.610
By the way, now that we've seen the film,
did you notice how in the very first teaser

00:11:14.610 --> 00:11:19.460
trailer Gunn foreshadowed Yondu's tragic death
with that shot of "spacesuits for emergency

00:11:19.460 --> 00:11:20.860
or for fun"?

00:11:20.860 --> 00:11:25.920
So do you think it was a good decision for
Yondu to die at the end of Vol. 2 and were

00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:29.310
there any deleted scenes or characters you
wish had been left in the movie?

00:11:29.310 --> 00:11:31.870
And what would you like to see in Vol. 3?

